```console
$ pnpm add {{ name }}
```

## A day in the life

```ts
import { greet } from '{{ name }}'

greet('world')                 // 'Hello, world!'
greet('Ada', { title: 'Dr' })  // 'Hello, Dr Ada!'
```

## Developing

```console
$ pnpm install
$ pnpm lint
```

`lint` is the whole gate - eslint, the packages' typecheck and the tests - so a
green terminal means a green pull request.
{{#stand}}

It **builds the package first**, and has to: the stand imports `{{ name }}` by
its published name, whose `exports` point at `dist`. With no build, the
typecheck fails on a module that is merely not compiled yet - which reads like
a broken import and is not one.
{{/stand}}

```console
$ pnpm build
```
{{#stand}}

```console
$ pnpm stand
```

The stand is a page that imports the package **by its published name**, which
resolves to the workspace member. What it renders is the built package, not its
source: if the build is broken, the stand is too, which is the point of having
one.
{{/stand}}

## Layout

| Path | What lives there |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/{{ name }}/` | The published package - the only thing that ships |
{{#stand}}
| `stand/` | A Vite page where the package is seen running |
{{/stand}}
| `tools/` | Build helpers the packages call |
| `docs/adr/` | Architecture decisions, newest last |

The repository root is `private: true`: it is a workspace, not a package, and
nothing at the root is publishable. Only what is under `packages/` goes to npm.

